Are you gonna make the work-in-progress publicly viewable so we
can ooh and aah and/or gnash our teeth and scream? It would be
nice to see it - for one thing, your "what not to include" question
has an awfully big answer, and it'd be nice if we could holler
about excess information on an ongoing basis.

I'd be pretty conservative generally. You want to make Nethack
less scary and confusing without making it less difficult. I started
without the guidebook (an old Slackware distribution that was either
missing it or pre-dated it), so it isn't strictly necessary at all.
Remember that whenever debating what hard information to include in
it.

I think the most important things to stress in the guidebook are:

(a) Your goal is to aquire the Amulet of Yendor and present it to
your god, and
(b) This is not going to be easy.

There's been some debate on the first point, and how specific it
should be. In my (experienced!) opinion, figuring out what to do
with the Amulet is much easier than figuring out, say, how to
find and open Moloch's Sanctum. I would leave the phrasing of the
goal the same as in the current guidebook.

I'm against tables of weapons, armor, and equipment, although
it might be worth it to present a plain-language description of
nonmagical starting items:

"Archeologists understand dungeons pretty well; this enables
them to move quickly and sneak up on the local nasties.

"Archeologist enter the dungeon wearing an old leather jacket and
a lucky fedora. He or she carries a bullwhip for driving away
enemies (and perhaps a few stunt scenes), a pickaxe for digging out
collapsed tunnels (and for dealing with more obstinate foes!), a
sack for storing delicate finds, a tinning kit for preserving meat
for later consumption, a touchstone for identification of valuables,
and plenty of food. Some archaeologists are better-prepared than
others, and yours might remember to bring a tin opener, a light
source, or a writing tool."

I would even go so far as to remove the -current- listing of armor
class by armor type.

I think the in-game help and look commands should be stressed
early and often, and the discussion on food should be put into
its own section rather than buried in the item types.

Also, suggestions on setting options are in my opinion appropriate.
At the beginning of the options section, a brief discussion on
commonly used and important options might preceed the flat list.

Finally, a far more general suggestion. The guidebook is part of
the game, and should be treated as such. In my opinion, the purpose
of the guidebook should not be to make the game easier (I would
delete all mention of Elbereth if it were up to me), but to make
the game less confusing. The focus of the guidebook should be on
helping the player interpret the game's output and on what action
(but not result!) will take place for a given command.

I'm very much against the idea of the guidebook including
"character knowledge", beyond the minimum necessary to play the
game (Yeah, you have a god, and you can #pray to contact it. Oh,
and there's a #offer command to offer a sacrifice, you might
try it some time.).

Nethack is far more a game of strategy, problem-solving, and
experimentation than of roleplaying, and every bit of information
in the Guidebook is something a new player is not going to have the
joy of figuring out.

(I think the guidebook should, however, have a mention of rgrn
for those who want the game spoiled.)

There, I've said my piece. The game I fell in love with was a strange
jumble of characters with a hundred fiddly little commands and no
clue where to go but down. I starved a lot; I escaped with a Cheap
Plastic Imitation and laughed my fool head off. I heard vague
whisperings of the name "Elbereth" but never thought to -write- it
until I got into the castle. I heard strange sounds of nymphs splashing
and mysterious noises; I abandoned my pet as a matter of course. It
was years before I stepped on a scary scroll and a magic trap at the
same time and worked that one out.

Okay, I'm still saying my piece, but I'm almost done. My point is
that for me, the excitement of being clueless was a large factor in
my enjoyment of the game, and I strongly feel that the Guidebook
should allow future players to experience this. Dying, horrifically,
a lot, and not always knowing why - that's a great deal of the fun
bit early on. It isn't just about the simple struggle for survival,
but the struggle to learn how to go about surviving.

